  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nToday-Tue...An upper level (cut-off) low will continue to spin \nover the western FL peninsula today then open up into a trough and \nlift NE on Tue. Deep moisture (PWATs 2-2.2\") will support \nscattered to numerous (50-70%) showers and storms both afternoons\nover land. Overnight/early morning showers and storms will\ncontinue over the Atlc, sparked this morning by a land breeze\nslowly pushing offshore. As diurnal heating develops, convection\nshould develop along the sea breeze by early afternoon, then \ndrift inland into the evening hours. Temps aloft remain cool today  \n(-8C at 500mb) so a few strong storms are possible containing \ngusty winds to 50 mph, frequent lightning and locally heavy \nrainfall 1-3\". Temps are forecast to warm to -6C Tue as the upper\nlow weakens/fills so risk of strong storms will decrease.  Highs \nin the upper 80s to lower 90s and high humidity will support peak\nafternoon heat indices in the upper 90s to near 100 degrees.\n\nWed-Sun...An upper level trough drops SE out of the Great Lakes \nregion Wed, then deepens into a strong cut-off low just off the\nmid Atlc coast late week with an assocd sfc low deepening near\n990mb. This stacked low pressure system will not move much as \nhigh pressure to the north will create a blocking pattern into the\nweekend. In the low levels, a cool front will get driven \nsouthward into the Florida peninsula Wednesday with a secondary \nsurge pushing the front south of the area by Friday.\n\nDeep moisture (PWATs 2-2.3\") will persist over the area Wed esp \nsouthern sections while the presence of the front maintains enough\nlift for numerous (60-70%) showers and storms, especially in the \nafternoons. N/NE winds are forecast to increase Thu as pressure \ngradient tightens supporting 10-15 mph and gusts 20-25 mph\nspreading southward from Volusia into Brevard during Thu. Gusts \nnear 30 mph are possible along the immediate Volusia coast \nThursday aftn-eve. Max temperatures in the upper 80s to lower 90s\ncontinue.\n\nDrier air will filter down into central FL beginning Fri, lowering \nPoPs to 20-40% this weekend. Breezy NE flow continues, especially \nalong the coast on Friday then decreasing slightly this weekend. \nDrier air and onshore flow will allow for high temperatures to \ndrop a few degrees to the mid to upper 80s. However, min temps \nwill remain quite mild holding in the 70s due to the onshore flow.\nBut as that onshore flow decreases, inland sections should drop \ninto the mid and upper 60s Sat night/Sunday morning.",
